Give IOKit a platform expert it can actually instantiate.

Out of the box this table offers exactly one driver for IOPlatformExpertDevice,
and that driver's whole job is to panic:

    bool
    IOPanicPlatform::start(IOService * provider)
    {
        ...
        panic("Unable to find driver for this platform: \"%s\".\n", platform_name);
    }

On a real device the match is won by AppleARMPlatform, which arrives as a
prelinked kext. A kernel built straight out of this tree has no kexts at all --
no __PRELINK_TEXT, no __PRELINK_INFO -- so nothing outbids IOPanicPlatform and
the boot ends there, at the first moment IOKit looks for a platform. Everything
before it is already working: zalloc, oslog, the workqueue tables and kdp_core
all come up, and StartIOKit() runs.

IOPlatformExpert itself is concrete and is the obvious stand-in. Its start()
does nothing this board would object to: it sets the IOMapper requirement from
a property that is absent, creates the interrupt-controller dictionary, fixes
up clock-frequency, installs itself as gIOPlatform, instantiates the power
domains, and hands off to configure(), which looks for a "top-level" property,
does not find one, and returns true.

IODTPlatformExpert would be the more faithful choice -- it is what walks the
device tree and publishes a nub per node -- but it is declared with
OSDeclareAbstractStructors and leaves deleteList() and excludeList() pure, so
it cannot be named from a personality. Subclassing it means a new source file
and the build plumbing to go with it; that is worth doing once there are
drivers for the nubs to match, and worth nothing before.

So this is deliberately the minimum that gets the boot past IOKit's first
question. It publishes no device nubs, and no driver will match anything,
because there are no drivers. What it buys is the next failure instead of this
one.

The score only has to beat IOPanicPlatform's zero; numbers in these tables are
decimal unless they start with 0x.

Note the comma. IOCatalogue::initialize() runs this through the legacy
OSUnserialize, whose grammar is

    elements: object | elements ',' object

so a second entry without one is a syntax error -- and the failure is not
graceful: the result is asserted non-NULL a few lines later, so IOKit would
come up with no personalities at all and panic. The table had one entry until
now, which is why it has never needed a separator.

--- a/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
+++ b/iokit/KernelConfigTables.cpp
@@ -35,6 +35,11 @@
 const char * gIOKernelConfigTables =
     "("
     "   {"
+    "     'IOClass'         = IOPlatformExpert;"
+    "     'IOProviderClass' = IOPlatformExpertDevice;"
+    "     'IOProbeScore'    = 1000:32;"
+    "   },"
+    "   {"
     "     'IOClass'         = IOPanicPlatform;"
     "     'IOProviderClass' = IOPlatformExpertDevice;"
     "     'IOProbeScore'    = 0:32;"
